        "ballot_id": "B022",
        "laureates": [
          "Brian J. Druker",
          "Nicholas B. Lydon",
          "Charles L. Sawyers"
        ],
        "citation": "for transforming chronic myeloid leukemia through inhibition of BCR-ABL"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B041",
    "B005"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B022 is a mature, bounded achievement in which molecular understanding of a defined cancer driver led to a highly effective treatment with sustained benefit in chronic myeloid leukemia and paradigm-setting influence on targeted oncology. Lydon, Druker, and Sawyers represent complementary drug-development, clinical-validation, and resistance/mechanistic contributions within that achievement.",
    "case_against": "The credit boundary remains the central reservation: BCR-ABL biology, medicinal chemistry, clinical development, and subsequent treatment advances form a long chain, and the proposed formulation must not inadvertently subsume earlier discovery of the fusion oncogene or wider contributions outside the three credited scientists.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "linnarsson-sten",
        "point": "Linnarsson ranked GLP-1 physiology first, emphasizing its extensive clinical impact, while noting that its candidate formulation spans hormone discovery, physiological definition, and drug development.",
        "response": "I agree that B005 has exceptional medical importance and is a strong alternative. Its distributed discovery-to-drug trajectory, however, makes a precise prize boundary and allocation of credit less clear than for B022."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "sandberg-rickard",
        "point": "Sandberg ranked chaperonin-assisted folding first because it is a fundamental, long-established cellular principle with a focused two-person attribution.",
        "response": "The clarity and conceptual depth of B029 are persuasive. I nevertheless give priority to B022 because it joins a defined molecular mechanism to a durable and transformative clinical proof of principle, while retaining a plausible three-person account of the therapeutic advance."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "wahren-herlenius-marie",
        "point": "Wahren-Herlenius emphasized the foundational and enduring importance of distinct B- and T-lymphocyte lineages.",
        "response": "B008 is a profound and mature discovery with compact attribution. For this configuration, B022 more directly captures a discrete mechanistic-to-therapeutic advance whose long-term clinical effect and broader influence on medicine are both firmly established."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the B022 citation can cleanly distinguish BCR-ABL inhibition from the earlier discovery of BCR-ABL biology.",
      "Whether the respective contributions of Lydon, Druker, and Sawyers sufficiently delimit the larger drug-development and clinical network."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
